12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the technical distinction between 'sensitivity' and 'specificity' in biomedical diagnostic tests.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Diagnostic Performance Metrics:) Tj
T*
(   In immunological assays \(ELISA, lateral flow\) and molecular diagnostics \(RT-qPCR\), tests are) Tj
T*
(   evaluated against two fundamental epidemiological parameters:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Formulations:) Tj
T*
(   - Diagnostic Sensitivity \(True Positive Rate\):) Tj
T*
(   - Ability of an assay to correctly identify individuals who HAVE the disease/target analyte:) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: Sensitivity = True Positives / \(True Positives + False Negatives\).) Tj
T*
(   - Clinical Meaning: High sensitivity means virtually zero false negatives; ideal for screening) Tj
T*
(   tests where missing a positive case is catastrophic.) Tj
T*
(   - Diagnostic Specificity \(True Negative Rate\):) Tj
T*
(   - Ability of an assay to correctly identify individuals who DO NOT HAVE the disease/target) Tj
T*
(   analyte:) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: Specificity = True Negatives / \(True Negatives + False Positives\).) Tj
T*
(   - Clinical Meaning: High specificity means virtually zero false positives; ideal for) Tj
T*
(   confirmatory tests where a false alarm causes severe distress.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Analytical Sensitivity vs. Specificity:) Tj
T*
(   - Analytical Sensitivity: Limit of Detection \(LoD - lowest detectable analyte concentration\).) Tj
T*
(   - Analytical Specificity: Ability to discriminate target sequence from homologous non-target) Tj
T*
(   sequences without cross-reactivity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
