(>> Solution to Question 24:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze Latin abbreviations in scientific text: 'i.e.' versus 'e.g.', and 'in situ' versus 'ex situ' conservation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. 'i.e.' versus 'e.g.':) Tj
T*
(   - i.e. \(id est - 'that is'\): Specifies, restates, or provides an exhaustive definition of the) Tj
T*
(   preceding term:) Tj
T*
(   - "The patient exhibited agranulocytosis \(i.e., a severe deficiency of neutrophils, basophils,) Tj
T*
(   and eosinophils\).") Tj
T*
(   - e.g. \(exempli gratia - 'for example'\): Introduces non-exhaustive, illustrative examples:) Tj
T*
(   - "Model eukaryotic organisms \(e.g., Saccharomyces cerevisiae, Drosophila melanogaster\) were) Tj
T*
(   utilized.") Tj
T*
(   - Punctuation: Both require periods and a following comma in American English: \(i.e., ...\) and) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., ...\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. 'In Situ' versus 'Ex Situ' Conservation in Biotechnology:) Tj
T*
(   - In Situ Conservation: Preserving endangered genetic resources, crops, or wild species within) Tj
T*
(   their native natural ecosystems:) Tj
T*
(   - Example: Biosphere reserves, national parks, wild protected habitats.) Tj
T*
(   - Ex Situ Conservation: Preserving genetic diversity outside native habitats in artificial) Tj
T*
(   biotechnological repositories:) Tj
T*
(   - Example: Cryogenic seed banks, liquid nitrogen germplasm repositories, tissue culture) Tj
T*
(   collections.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 25:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine phrasal verbs with 'give' \('give up', 'give off', 'give in', 'give rise to'\) in biological descriptions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Formal Latinate Transformations:) Tj
T*
(   - Give off:) Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: Producing gas, odor, heat, or light.) Tj
T*
(   - Formal Replacement: Emit, release, radiate, discharge.) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "Anaerobic fermentation emitted substantial volumes of carbon dioxide and hydrogen) Tj
T*
ET

